<!--
    repipe-31-byte-buffer-copy: "31-byte buffer copy loses its middle fifteen
    bytes"
    docs/re-needs/31-byte-buffer-copy.md

    A crackme copies a 31-byte stack buffer into another with the compiler's
    standard idiom for a size that is not a multiple of the word: four 8-byte
    moves at offsets 0, 8, 15 and 23, so the middle two overlap on byte 15.
    kuna emitted the first and the last and nothing in between, so the C copied
    bytes 0..7 and 23..30 and left the fifteen between them never written.

    P3 heritage.  RuleStoreVarnode rewrites a frame STORE to a direct
    `stackvn = COPY(value)` and marks the output `stack_store`.  That mark is
    load-bearing: ActionDirectWrite calls a COPY into the frame a direct write
    only when its output isStackStore(), and ActionDeadCode opens by dropping
    the addrforce mark of anything that is not a direct write, after which
    nothing consumes the store and the COPY is swept.  Heritage::refinement cuts
    every access in an overlapping range to a common partition, and
    Heritage::refineWrite rebuilds the store's storage out of SUBPIECEs whose
    cells splitByRefinement creates with plain newVarnode and they carry no mark.
    So exactly the stores that overlap lose their addrforce and are deleted,
    while the unrefined ones beside them survive.

    Fix: `option splitstorekeep` (default on) copies the mark from the write
    being refined onto its partition cells.  A piece of a store is a store.

    Source (as(1), x86-64, linked -Ttext=0x100000; fixture
    decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/splitstorekeep_x86_64.s):

        copy31: sub $0x58,%rsp          ; src at rsp+0, dst at rsp+0x20
                mov %rsp,%rdi ; call sink    ; src escapes, so nothing is dead
                mov (%rsp),%rax             ; the witness quartet, verbatim
                mov 0x8(%rsp),%rdx
                mov %rax,0x20(%rsp)         ; [0,8)
                mov %rdx,0x28(%rsp)         ; [8,16)
                mov 0xf(%rsp),%rax
                mov 0x17(%rsp),%rdx
                mov %rax,0x2f(%rsp)         ; [15,23)  ; overlaps [8,16)
                mov %rdx,0x37(%rsp)         ; [23,31)
                lea 0x20(%rsp),%rdi ; call sink
                add $0x58,%rsp ; ret
        copy32: the same shape at 32 bytes, so the four moves tile [0,32) and
                none of them overlaps.  The control: refinement never fires and
                its C is identical in both passes.
        sink:   ret

    Pass 1 (default): the refined partition is 8 + 7 + 1 + 7 + 8, so copy31 is
    five assignments and the two seven-byte cells and the one-byte cell they
    overlap on are variables in their own right (#1 to #3).  Pass 2
    (splitstorekeep off): #5 reproduces the filed bug so the head store, then
    the tail store, and nothing for the fifteen bytes between them.
-->
